0..77 Comment "// A thunk crosses by its image: what it captures, and the declarations homed"
78..140 Comment "// at its world that it names. No former marks a mobile thunk."
141..143 Comment "//"
144..219 Comment "// A thunk is code over an environment, and the environment is what crosses"
220..293 Comment "// with it. Its image is read where it is formed, so a thunk crosses when"
294..356 Comment "// every capture and every homed declaration it names crosses."
357..359 Comment "//"
360..437 Comment "// Each declaration forms a thunk at the seat and returns it to the operator."
438..513 Comment "// The last two force it or apply it there, so it runs as it runs in place."
514..590 Comment "// The thunks are closed, or over an integer, a marked report or a handle to"
591..668 Comment "// an integer. The image\'s three axes are the capture rules, so every capture"
669..728 Comment "// stands at no region, at ω and at the thunk\'s own world."
729..731 Comment "//"
732..803 Comment "// - see: #3265 — `forced` and `applied`, a thunk run at the operator"
804..885 Comment "// - see: `pathological/worlds/closure-axes.gandr` — what a thunk captures that"
886..896 Comment "//   stays"
897..971 Comment "// - see: Epstein, Black and Peyton Jones, \"Towards Haskell in the Cloud\","
972..1043 Comment "//   Haskell 2011 — a closure crosses as its code and its environment"
1045..1119 Comment "// The operator\'s and the seat\'s worlds, and the seat\'s sealed side of the"
1120..1164 Comment "// exchange, its report alone marked mobile."
